1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:05,310
Cherry turns a lesson into
supervised work an agent can run.

2
00:00:05,310 --> 00:00:12,630
This is the deployed product. One task,
and an entire team of agents behind it.

3
00:00:12,630 --> 00:00:19,010
Two tasks. Two work areas. Thirty-four
seconds in parallel. Two checks passed.

4
00:00:19,010 --> 00:00:24,040
You set the goal. Cherry plans,
runs it, and checks the result.

5
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:29,350
The receipt: two agents ran
one job. Neither could publish it.

6
00:00:29,350 --> 00:00:36,740
A recorded run, verified
before it was ever put on this page.

7
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:43,140
Every step is on the record:
what ran, and what it produced.

8
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:48,180
Publishing stops here, because
no person had approved it yet.

9
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:54,350
Now WebMCP. Cherry hands a
visiting agent its tools from the page.

10
00:00:54,350 --> 00:01:02,730
Site tools available: eleven. Seven always on, and
stage tools that appear only when the state earns them.

11
00:01:02,730 --> 00:01:10,130
One row per registered closure. These are
the real tools, not a description of them.

12
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:15,150
And a live call log,
showing exactly what the agent ran.

13
00:01:15,150 --> 00:01:21,280
A real host did this too:
ChatGPT desktop, in Work mode.

14
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:28,700
It fetched the aperture and called the
tools. That row says Validated because it happened.

15
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:33,730
What ships, says ships.
What does not, says that too.

16
00:01:33,730 --> 00:01:42,960
Every claim here is checkable. And the one
thing an agent cannot do is approve its own work.
